# `where a, b` joins the conditions with `and` (M1-19).
mode: syntax
---- query
from T | where a > 1, b < 2, c | take 1
---- expected
PROGRAM@0..40
  PIPELINE@0..39
    IDENT@0..4 "from"
    WHITESPACE@4..5 " "
    NAME_REF@5..6
      IDENT@5..6 "T"
    WHITESPACE@6..7 " "
    PIPE@7..8 "|"
    WHITESPACE@8..9 " "
    WHERE_OP@9..30
      IDENT@9..14 "where"
      WHITESPACE@14..15 " "
      BIN_EXPR@15..20
        NAME_REF@15..16
          IDENT@15..16 "a"
        WHITESPACE@16..17 " "
        GT@17..18 ">"
        WHITESPACE@18..19 " "
        LITERAL@19..20
          LONG@19..20 "1"
      COMMA@20..21 ","
      WHITESPACE@21..22 " "
      BIN_EXPR@22..27
        NAME_REF@22..23
          IDENT@22..23 "b"
        WHITESPACE@23..24 " "
        LT@24..25 "<"
        WHITESPACE@25..26 " "
        LITERAL@26..27
          LONG@26..27 "2"
      COMMA@27..28 ","
      WHITESPACE@28..29 " "
      NAME_REF@29..30
        IDENT@29..30 "c"
    WHITESPACE@30..31 " "
    PIPE@31..32 "|"
    WHITESPACE@32..33 " "
    TAKE_OP@33..39
      IDENT@33..37 "take"
      WHITESPACE@37..38 " "
      LITERAL@38..39
        LONG@38..39 "1"
  WHITESPACE@39..40 "\n"

error[DS0115]: `where` takes one condition
  --> query:1:21
  |
1 | from T | where a > 1, b < 2, c | take 1
  |                     ^ a comma does not join conditions
  |
  = help: combine the conditions with `and`: `a > 1 and b < 2 and c`

with the machine-applicable fixes applied:
    from T | where a > 1 and b < 2 and c | take 1
which parses without errors
